WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.900
We have been given this rectangular grid of paths with this being

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.760
the starting point and this being the destination.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.900
Zara's at the starting point and wants to reach the cake shop

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.920
which is at the destination.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.660
Think of these lines as paths Zara can take to travel to the cake shop.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.900
With this, we have been asked for the number of different shortest paths

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.580
Zara can take, to reach the cake shop.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.740
This is the typical permutations and combinations problem

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.260
you see in the competitive exams.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.020
Sometimes we don't even realize that it's

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.780
a permutations and combinations problem.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.120
Are we asked to select or arrange objects?

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.520
No! Then how is it a permutations and combinations problem?

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.840
Read the question again! The number of shortest paths!

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.320
Shortest is the key word here.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.920
We have been asked to select the shortest path.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.040
How do we find the number of shortest paths?

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.740
Before you start thinking, let me tell you

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.840
the worst thing you could possibly do.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.500
Manually count the shortest paths.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.880
Don't even think of making that mistake

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.800
as you will end up losing a lot of time.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.340
Now to find the shortest paths we first need to understand

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.360
what the shortest path is.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.360
Many students assume that going to the right

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.200
and then straight up is one of the shortest paths.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.960
And the other is going straight up to the right to reach the destination.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.500
These are the two shortest paths students directly think of.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.460
They assume all other paths are longer.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.500
To know if this is true let's take a random path from inside.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:49.540
Right up, right up, right up, right up and right.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.060
We covered nine units lengths when we took this path.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.460
Let's take another random path.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:00.980
Up, up, right, right, up, right, up, right, right.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.020
Again nine units.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.880
Now let's look at the earlier paths

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.980
that had crossed our minds as the shortest paths.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.980
Right, right ,right, right, right, up, up, up and up.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.140
Again nine units,

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:21.940
We had also thought of up, up, up, up, right, right, right, right and right.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.200
The shortest path will cover nine units.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.060
That's our conclusion.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.620
We will come back to this point,

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:33.380
but let's answer a question that some of you all would be wondering about.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.800
What is not a shortest path?

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:40.340
The answer is simple! At any point if you travel left

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.360
or down it will not be a shortest path.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:49.720
So you go right, up, left, up, up, up, right, right, right, right, and right.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:54.080
This will be 11 units and it's clearly longer than 9.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.520
It's because we took a left here.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.260
The same logic applies if we go down.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.940
Up, up, right, down, right, right, right, right, up, up, and up.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.560
11 units more than 9.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.540
So for the shortest paths,

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:13.840
we can either travel right or up, not left or down.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.300
Now going back to a previous point, the shortest part will cover 9 units.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.740
What is this 9? It is the sum of the number of

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.200
columns and the number of rows.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.960
Yes, this grid has 5 columns.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:34.540
1, 2, 3, 4 &amp; 5. And it has 4 rows 1, 2, 3, &amp; 4.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.900
5 plus 4 is 9.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.620
So now you know what a shortest path is!

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.460
Now let's move on to solving the problem.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.880
In every case we saw Zara had to move 5 units to the right and 4 units up.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.880
The sum of these two is 9 units.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.640
So looking at this, can we say that out of the nine units

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.220
Zara travels to reach the destination,

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:00.300
five have to be rightwards?

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.980
Yes, we can say that, out of the nine units for any shortest path

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.880
we have to select five which go to the right.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.120
So '9 C 5' will give us the number of shortest paths

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.220
Zara can take to reach the destination.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.540
Wait, can we also say that out of the nine units

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.540
Zara takes for the shortest path,

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.480
4 have to be upwards?

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.860
Yes, '9 C 4' will also give us the same answer.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.180
These two will be equal.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.880
In case you're unable to recollect, the formula for 'N C R' is

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:39.000
n factorial divided by R factorial times N minus R factorial.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:45.880
This will equal 9 factorial divided by 5 factorial times 9 minus 5 factorial.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:52.760
And this will be 9 factorial divided by 4 factorial times 9 minus 4 factorial.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.120
Same answer!

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:59.980
126 different shortest paths Zara can take to reach the destination.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.520
Okay, we have the answer now!

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:06.120
But is there another interesting way to arrive at this answer?

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.160
Can we use another approach we have used before?

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.080
Think!

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.700
Could you think of anything?

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:22.100
Okay, so we know that Zara will be moving 5 units to the right

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.020
and 4 units upwards.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.720
We know that for sure!

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:30.260
No matter which shortest path she takes, it will be total of 9 units

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.520
out of which 5 will be to the right,

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.360
and 4 will be upwards.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:38.640
So we write  'R' five times and 'U' four times.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.180
This is what we need to arrange.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.220
All possible arrangements of these nine letters

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.260
will give us the number of different shortest paths Zara can take.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:52.040
This should remind you of the anagram problem we had solved.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.500
We had the word 'excessive'.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.540
As there were nine letters we wrote 9 factorial.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:02.600
E was present thrice, hence divided this by 3 factorial.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:07.580
S was present twice hence divided again by 2 factorial.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.860
We applied the same logic here,

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.200
As there are nine letters present,

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.140
we write nine factorial.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.760
'R' appears five times so divide this by '5' factorial.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:23.540
And as 'U' appears four times we divide this by '4 factorial'.

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.100
This also gives us the same answer.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:30.240
All three give us the answer as 126.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:35.880
Zara can take 126 different shortest paths to reach the cake shop.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.200
You may use any method you like,

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.600
depends on what you are comfortable with.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.400
But make sure you understand the concept well .

